-text-box">Artificial intelligence is changing how institutions make decisi=
ons about people. It is changing how people work, what they are exposed to,=
 and how their most sensitive information is used. The pace of that change =
has outrun the frameworks designed to protect the people inside it.
=20
My background was built for precisely this gap, even before the gap had a n=
ame.
=20
For eighteen years, I worked inside human systems under pressure, in crises=
, in trauma work, and in moments where institutional breakdown revealed exa=
ctly how fragile the distance is between a flawed system and a damaged life=
. That work taught me something that does not show up in technical audits o=
r compliance frameworks: the damage that happens when systems move faster t=
han the people inside them is not random. It follows patterns. It is recogn=
izable. And it is preventable if someone knows what to look for.
=20
I am a licensed clinical counsellor now turning that lens toward AI. I am c=
urrently pursuing advanced AI governance certifications at the internationa=
l level and building a consulting practice focused on the human cost of get=
ting AI wrong inside institutions, the part most governance frameworks do n=
ot yet adequately address.
=20
At the center of this work is a proprietary assessment framework I am devel=
oping to help organizations identify human systems risks before they become=
 liabilities, and build practices that are genuinely accountable to the peo=
ple they affect.
=20
My focus is on sectors where that accountability gap is widest: law enforce=
ment, government, Indigenous governance organizations, corporate enterprise=
s, and Alberta's energy sector.
=20
If you are navigating how your institution deploys AI and you want a perspe=
ctive grounded in human systems rather than just policy and compliance, I w=
elcome that conversation.</span></p></div></div></div></section></div></div=
