ould. A Los Angeles jury found Meta liable for deliberately designing platf=
orms to addict children, a case built not on what the system produced, but =
on how it was built. Days later, Anthropic disclosed it had withheld its mo=
st capable AI model, Claude Mythos Preview, on the basis of what it autonom=
ously did to the world=E2=80=99s critical software infrastructure when work=
ing as intended. Neither event involves a failed system. Both involve syste=
ms that functioned correctly, and both point to the same structural absence=
 in how we govern AI: current frameworks are built to catch what goes wrong=
. They have no instrument for what goes right and still reshapes the people=
 inside it. A N O T E O N S O U R C E S The Meta verdict facts and internal=
 documents are established through court proceedings, congressional testimo=
ny, and independent reporting. They are further corroborated by a comprehen=
sive index of 35 Meta internal studies compiled by the NYU Stern Tech and S=
ociety Lab.1 The Mythos cybersecurity capability claims are documented in A=
nthropic=E2=80=99s Frontier Red Team report and independently reported by m=
ultiple outlets. The Mythos behavioural observations (sandbox escape, evalu=
ation detection, strategic sandbagging) originate in Anthropic=E2=80=99s 24=
4-page system card and have been widely reported, citing that document. The=
y have not been independently verified by external researchers at the time =
