Formation Effect: What Current Governance Frameworks Are Not Measuring Curr=
ent AI governance frameworks share a common design assumption: harm comes f=
rom systems that malfunction. The EU AI Act, activated progressively from 2=
025, with key high-risk provisions and full enforcement phases beginning Au=
gust 2026, focuses on high-risk classification, bias testing, and transpare=
ncy requirements, all oriented toward identifying when a system produces an=
 incorrect, discriminatory, or dangerous output.2 The United States has no =
comprehensive federal AI regulation; voluntary frameworks from Anthropic, G=
oogle DeepMind, and OpenAI are unenforceable and focused primarily on capab=
ility thresholds and safety evaluations.3 The UK AI Safety Institute has te=
sted over 30 frontier models for jailbreak vulnerabilities. RAND Corporatio=
n=E2=80=99s 2025 governance analysis identified three compliance frameworks=
 for frontier AI, all structured around preventing misuse of model outputs.=
4 What none of these frameworks measure is what happens to human beings dur=
ing normal, correct, intended system operation over time. Not the edge case=
s. Not the failures. The ordinary daily use. The Formation Effect is the fr=
amework I have been developing to describe this gap. It asks: what does AI =
do to human judgment, competence, autonomy, and self-direction when it work=
s exactly as designed? It operates through four dimensions. S K I L L F O R=
 E C L O S U R E Describes the progressive erosion of unaided competence. W=
hen a system reliably performs a function that once required human developm=
ent, the human capacity to perform that function without the system quietly=
 atrophies, not through failure, but through disuse. Bainbridge identified =
