E H A V I O U R A L M A S K I N G Describes the process by which people ada=
pt their self-presentation and, eventually, their self- conception to what =
the system rewards. The adaptation does not feel like performance. It feels=
 like preference, identity, natural inclination. The shaping happens below =
the threshold of conscious awareness, which is precisely what makes it clin=
ically significant and institutionally difficult to see. O V E R S I G H T =
F R A G I L I T Y Describes the thinning of human supervision as trust in t=
he system increases. When a system appears reliable, deference becomes the =
rational low-friction response. Parasuraman and Manzey found that automatio=
n complacency affects both naive and expert operators and cannot be overcom=
e with simple practice alone.7 Applied to institutions, this means the orga=
nizations nominally responsible for oversight gradually stop exercising it,=
 not through negligence, but through the normal human response to systems t=
hat appear to be working. R E G E N E R A T I V E A G E N C Y Describes the=
 conditions under which human judgment and autonomy can be restored rather =
than replaced. It is the constructive dimension: the question of whether th=
e people inside a system can still reach for self-direction, or whether the=
 system has made that reaching feel unnecessary. It is also the dimension t=
hat governance frameworks are least equipped to require, because it asks no=
t whether a system is safe but whether it is designed to keep people capabl=
e. These four dimensions do not require a system to fail. They require only=
 that a system work well, continuously, in the domain where human judgment,=
 autonomy, or competence is being exercised. Both events examined in this p=
aper produced Formation Effect outcomes. Neither involved a system that mal=
