 content liability. Meta was held liable not for what users posted but for =
how the platform was engineered. This distinction matters for governance: i=
t represents the first successful application of product liability law to a=
lgorithmic design, bypassing the Section 230 immunity that had previously s=
hielded platforms from accountability for user outcomes.11 What Meta Knew a=
nd What It Did Meta=E2=80=99s internal research was extensive and precise. =
Thirty-two percent of teen girls reported Instagram made body image issues =
worse. Thirteen-and-a-half percent of teen girls in the UK reported it wors=
ened thoughts of suicide. Seventeen percent said it worsened eating disorde=
rs.12 Project Mercury, an internal deactivation study, found users who stop=
ped using Instagram for one week reported measurably lower depression, anxi=
ety, and loneliness.13 These findings are independently corroborated by pee=
r-reviewed research: Valkenburg et al. (2022) found consistent negative ass=
ociations between social media use and adolescent mental health across 25 r=
eviews; Khalaf et al. (2023) found statistically significant associations b=
etween social media use and depression in adolescents across 11 studies.14 =
The NYU Stern Tech and Society Lab has compiled a public index of 35 Meta i=
nternal studies documenting the company=E2=80=99s own knowledge of these ha=
rms.1 All 18 of Meta=E2=80=99s internal well-being experts agreed Instagram=
 should prohibit beauty filters linked to body dysmorphia. Zuckerberg decli=
ned. Nick Clegg emailed Zuckerberg warning the company was not on track to =
address well-being failures. No reply. Arturo B=C3=A9jar, former engineerin=
g director, emailed Zuckerberg about a critical gap in how the company appr=
oached harm. No reply. B=C3=A9jar testified before Congress: =E2=80=9CMark=
=E2=80=99s level of proof, in order to be able to take the work seriously a=
nd act on it, is too high.=E2=80=9D15 Meta knew default-private settings fo=
r teens would have eliminated 5.4 million unwanted interactions per day. Th=
ey did not implement them.16 The Formation Effect Dimensions in This Case E=
ach of the four dimensions is present in what the jury named =E2=80=94 and =
what the jury could not name, because no governance framework had instrumen=
ts for it. Skill Foreclosure The competence that was foreclosed in these ch=
ildren was emotional discernment, the developing capacity to identify and p=
rocess authentic feeling without algorithmic mediation. Instagram did not p=
revent children from feeling. It flooded them with engineered emotional sti=
muli calibrated for maximum engagement, gradually displacing the internal r=
egulatory capacity that develops through unmediated experience. Clinically,=
 this is not dramatic. It does not announce itself. A child who has spent t=
hree years having their emotional responses shaped by an engagement algorit=
hm does not experience this as loss. They experience it as normal." src=3D"=
