x">We have frameworks for what AI does wrong. We don't have frameworks for =
what AI does to people when it works exactly as designed.<br><br>This verdi=
ct is what that looks like in a courtroom.<br><br>A Los Angeles jury just f=
ound Meta and Google liable for deliberately designing platforms to addict =
children. The plaintiff started using YouTube at age 6 and Instagram at age=
 9. By the time she finished elementary school, she had developed anxiety, =
depression, and suicidal ideation. Internal documents showed Meta knew. One=
 memo said they needed to bring children in as tweens to win with teens. An=
other showed 11-year-olds were four times more likely to return to Instagra=
m than competing apps, despite the platform's own age minimum of 13.<br><br=
>They had the data. And they kept building.<br><br>Then, after the verdict,=
 Meta pulled ads from its own platforms that were recruiting plaintiffs for=
 addiction lawsuits =E2=80=94 ads that highlighted how children were being =
targeted and harmed. Meta removed them.<br><br>If you can remove something =
that quickly, you chose not to before.<br>That's not a bug. That's the arch=
itecture.<br><br>This is The Formation Effect in motion. What happens when =
a system works exactly as designed and still quietly reshapes the people in=
side it? Not through failure, but through function. Meta did not malfunctio=
n. It optimized for engagement and dependency, and in doing so, it graduall=
y eroded the judgment, emotional regulation, and self-trust of children.<br=
><br>The four dimensions I've been mapping =E2=80=94 Skill Foreclosure, Beh=
avioural Masking, Oversight Fragility, and Regenerative Agency =E2=80=94 ar=
e all present in what this jury named. The system didn't create the vulnera=
bility. It found it, amplified it, and built a product around it. The court=
s and a jury named it before any governance framework did. That's not a vic=
tory. It's a warning.<br><br>Thank you,<span class=3D"_6b4e6858"> </span><a=
