andable-text-box">We have frameworks for what AI does wrong.<br><br>We stil=
l don't have strong ones for what it does to people when it works exactly a=
s designed.<br><br>After nearly two decades inside high-stakes systems, cri=
sis settings, clinical practice, and institutional environments, I have wat=
ched how repetition, reinforcement, and convenience quietly remodel people-=
 not through dramatic failure, but through ordinary, frictionless use.<br><=
br>AI doesn't create new problems from scratch. It powerfully amplifies and=
 reshapes whatever already exists in people and institutions...for better a=
nd for worse. What makes this moment different from every previous automati=
on wave: AI has moved into the most intimate cognitive and relational terri=
tory that humans occupy. These relational and reflective functions used to =
require the involvement of another human being with genuine stakes in the o=
utcome. In contrast, AI can deliver this immediately, and without any stake=
, accountability, consequence, or investment.<br><br>Bainbridge identified =
this decades ago: when machines absorb the cognitive work, humans gradually=
 lose the capacity to step back meaningfully when those systems fail. We ar=
e watching this happen inside the most judgment-dependent, relationship-dep=
endent work we do, at scale and in real time.<br>The result isn't incompete=
nce. It's something quieter: people who function well with the system and l=
ess well without it. People who reach for external guidance before they've =
reached inward, outsourcing the friction that used to build them.<br><br>Wh=
at happens to human capacity, institutional integrity, and decision-making =
when an uninvested algorithm becomes the most readily available source of g=
uidance? <br><br>That doesn't show up in audits. It shows up in people.<br>=
<br>Parasuraman and Riley showed how oversight quietly thins as systems app=
ear reliable; deference becomes the rational, low-friction response long be=
fore anyone names it as a problem. Governance built to detect system failur=
es wasn't designed to see this. It's not a failure. It's a system working e=
xactly as designed, reshaping the people inside it. And that's what makes i=
t so hard to govern, because nothing has gone wrong.<br><br>None of this fe=
els like harm. It feels like help.<br><br>This sits at the center of govern=
ance, risk, clinical practice, and institutional leadership- whether or not=
 it has been named yet. That's what makes it significant across every domai=
n where human judgment still matters, and is why The Formation Effect is re=
levant: to make visible what it costs people and institutions when AI works=
 exactly as designed, early enough to act on it.<br><br>Over the coming wee=
ks, I'll be unpacking its four dimensions, beginning with Skill Foreclosure=
.<br><br>The people already sensing this-in their institutions, their commu=
nities, and in the longer arc of what we are collectively becoming and wher=
e we are headed-are exactly who this conversation is for.<br><br>Where are =
