t-box">We have frameworks for what AI does wrong.<br><br>We don't have fram=
eworks for what AI does to people when it works exactly as designed.<br><br=
>That's the gap nobody in the governance conversation is talking about.<br>=
<br>I've spent nearly two decades working with people inside systems that w=
ere failing them, in classrooms, in crisis settings, and in therapy rooms, =
watching how environments shape people. Not dramatically. Not all at once. =
Slowly, through repetition, through what gets reinforced and what gets igno=
red. What I'm observing now is this: people are increasingly turning to AI =
for something that used to require another human being: judgment, emotional=
 guidance, validation, perspective.<br><br>And AI delivers those things rea=
dily, immediately, and without friction. But here's what it cannot deliver:=
 genuine investment in who you become. Accountability for the advice it giv=
es you. Any real understanding of your history, your context, or what you a=
ctually need. Real consequences if it gets it wrong.<br><br>What happens wh=
en people repeatedly turn to something that feels like support but has none=
 of those things?<br><br>They stop trusting themselves to know. They outsou=
rce decisions they used to make internally. They process emotions through a=
 system that has never once had anything at stake in their life. They build=
 dependency on something that is optimizing for their engagement, not their=
 growth.<br><br>None of this feels like harm. It feels like help.<br><br>Th=
at's what makes it clinically significant, and it's precisely why current g=
overnance frameworks aren't designed to catch it. They are built to identif=
y system failures. This isn't a system failure. It's a system working exact=
ly as intended, quietly reshaping the people who use it.<br><br>The questio=
n institutions aren't asking yet is not: is this AI safe to use?<br><br>It'=
s: what happens to human judgment, autonomy, and self-reliance when an unin=
vested algorithm becomes the most consistently available source of guidance=
 in a person's life?<br><br>That question doesn't show up in audits.<br><br=
