tive">
    <span dir=3D"ltr"><!---->The Oversight Fragility dimension is the one t=
hat lands hardest from a clinical perspective. In thirty years of practice =
I've watched supervisory judgment thin gradually as clinicians defer more t=
o tools, protocols, and systems they've learned to trust. It doesn't happen=
 because the clinician stops caring. It happens because the system is relia=
ble enough that active oversight starts to feel redundant. The Mythos case =
is that dynamic operating at a speed and scale that makes the thinning visi=
ble overnight rather than across years. In clinical settings it accumulates=
 slowly enough that nobody names it until something breaks. The question La=
uren is raising =E2=80=94 what are the instruments for catching success-as-=
harm before it becomes a crisis =E2=80=94 is one clinical governance has ne=
ver fully answered either.<!----></span>
</div>
